() Tj
T*
(LONG ANSWER BANK) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(EXPLAIN THE PROJECT END-TO-END) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(The system is a CMS-style web platform. Admin users authenticate into an authoring ) Tj
T*
(application and edit pages through a visual page builder. The editor stores content as ) Tj
T*
(structured layout data composed of sections, rows, columns, and blocks. Draft changes are ) Tj
T*
(autosaved and manually saveable as revisions rather than being applied directly to the live ) Tj
T*
(site. When a page is previewed, the system loads draft revision data and renders it through ) Tj
T*
(the shared layout and bundle pipeline. When a page is published, the current draft is ) Tj
T*
(validated for public use, stored as a published revision, and referenced as the live version) Tj
T*
( of that page. On the read side, the public website requests the page bundle by slug, the ) Tj
T*
(backend assembles page metadata, the published revision, and any global content like ) Tj
T*
(header/footer/banner, validates the canonical render bundle, and the website server-renders ) Tj
T*
(the result. The design intentionally separates writing, interpretation, and rendering ) Tj
T*
(responsibilities to reduce drift and improve lifecycle safety.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(EXPLAIN THE FRONTEND ARCHITECTURE END-TO-END) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(The admin frontend is a rich React editor built around local state and custom hooks. It ) Tj
T*
(loads revision-backed layout data, renders it dynamically through a registry, and supports ) Tj
T*
(interactions like drag-and-drop, property editing, autosave, undo/redo, preview, version ) Tj
T*
(history, and template application. Its biggest strength is workflow grouping through hooks, ) Tj
T*
(and its biggest weakness is orchestration density in the top-level editor container. The ) Tj
T*
(public frontend is much thinner. It uses SSR to fetch a validated page bundle, derive a ) Tj
T*
(public page view, and render a stable page shell. The public app intentionally avoids ) Tj
T*
(becoming a content interpretation layer.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(EXPLAIN THE BACKEND ARCHITECTURE END-TO-END) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(The backend is built around Next.js route handlers, services, repositories, shared ) Tj
T*
(validation, and MySQL persistence. Sensitive routes are protected through middleware and ) Tj
T*
(server-side auth checks. Save and publish workflows are handled in services, not in raw ) Tj
T*
(route handlers, because they involve validation, transactions, revision creation, and page-) Tj
ET
endstream
endobj
